{
  "requirement": "The mdBook preprocessor MUST report an error when a generated evidence detail page would use the path of an existing book chapter.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Implementation.** `DetailPlan::find` in `src/index.rs` (lines 266–321) is marked `{impl}` for `requirements.index.path_conflicts`. The check itself has its own `{impl}` marker. It does the following:\n- It computes the planned detail path for every definition with `detail_path`.\n- It collects the paths of all existing book chapters, using `book.chapters()` and `chapter.path`.\n- If any planned detail path is in that set, it returns `invalid_index(\"generated evidence page <path> conflicts with a book chapter\")`.\n\n`preprocess_book` in `src/lib.rs` calls `DetailPlan::find(...)?` before `populate`, so the error propagates out of the preprocessor and no chapters are modified. That meets the requirement that the preprocessor report an error. Detail pages are only inserted in `populate`, which runs after the check, so a conflicting page is never silently written over a chapter.\n\nThe same loop also errors when two generated detail paths collide with each other, using `!generated.insert(path)`. The requirement does not name this case, but a generated page is itself a book chapter once inserted. The check is therefore a reasonable reading of \"the path of an existing book chapter\", and it sits under the same `{impl}` marker. The message wording \"conflicts with a book chapter\" still fits. I do not count it as an unmarked extra behavior.\n\n**Tests.** `generated_detail_path_cannot_replace_a_chapter` (`src/index.rs`, lines 1416–1446) is marked `{test}`. It builds a book with an existing chapter at `requirements/requirements.ready.md`. It also defines `requirements.ready` in `requirements.md`, so the generated page would land on that same path. It asserts that `DetailPlan::find` returns an error containing \"conflicts with a book chapter\". This covers the failure case, and the other tests show the same plan succeeds without a conflict. A test for generated-versus-generated collisions would be an optional extra, because the requirement does not specify that case.\n\nI found no gaps and no FIXMEs.",
